This session helped me understand that finding an AI opportunity is not about simply finding a place where AI can be used.
The first question should always be whether AI actually creates meaningful value.
I liked the point that customers will not pay extra just because we add the word “AI” to a product.
It made me think about how easy it is to get excited about technology without checking the actual problem we are solving.
The discussion on Jobs to Be Done was also interesting because it forces us to think from the customer's perspective.
Instead of asking what feature we can build, we should ask what job the customer is trying to complete.
AI should ideally make that job easier, faster, cheaper or better.
Another important learning for me was that an opportunity needs to be looked at from both value and feasibility.
Something can have huge potential but still not be technically or economically practical today.
I also found the discussion about funding learning, and not only ROI, very interesting.

The build, buy or API decision also made me think differently about AI sourcing.
As a product manager, I should not only think about the model, but also about dependency, vendor control, cost and the capability that I actually want to own.

The product has to communicate confidence, uncertainty and when human intervention is required.
The discussion on human-in-the-loop was probably the most interesting part for me.
Simply putting a human in the process does not automatically make the system safe if the person starts blindly accepting AI recommendations.

biggest takeaway is that a good AI product is not about using more AI; it is about using AI where it genuinely improves the customer's 
job and creates measurable value.
